<main>
<h1>Homework 2: from a wallet seed to an Ethereum address</h1>
<p class="muted">Statistics 2026. How a 12-word recovery phrase becomes an Ethereum address, and what each cryptographic step does.</p>

<h2>1. Basic ideas</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Key generation</b> creates a secret from fresh randomness. In a wallet this happens once, when the 128 random bits are drawn.</li>
<li><b>Key derivation</b> computes new keys deterministically from an existing secret. The same input always gives the same keys. PBKDF2 and HMAC-SHA512 are used for this.</li>
<li><b>Public-key cryptography</b> uses a key pair. The public key can be shared, and the private key signs. Getting the private key from the public key is hard (elliptic-curve discrete logarithm).</li>
<li><b>Hashing</b> turns any input into a fixed-size digest, with no key. It is one-way (preimage resistance), it is hard to find a second input with the same digest, and hard to find any collision.</li>
<li><b>Checksums</b> catch typing mistakes. They add no secrecy.</li>
<li><b>Encoding</b> only changes the representation (words, hexadecimal, letter case). It does not hide or add information.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. Historical context</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Technology</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1977</td><td>RSA</td><td>First widely used public-key cryptosystem, based on factoring.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2001</td><td>SHA-2 / SHA-512</td><td>NIST hash standard. SHA-512 is used inside PBKDF2 and HMAC here.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2012</td><td>BIP-32</td><td>Hierarchical deterministic wallets: one seed gives a tree of keys.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2013</td><td>BIP-39</td><td>Mnemonic words, and the seed made from them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2014</td><td>BIP-44</td><td>Standard derivation paths for several coins and accounts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2015</td><td>SHA-3</td><td>NIST standard based on Keccak (FIPS 202).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2016</td><td>EIP-55</td><td>Mixed-case checksum for Ethereum addresses.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>SHA-2 / SHA-512 versus Keccak / SHA-3</h3>
<p>SHA-2 hashes the message block by block with a compression function. Keccak is a sponge: the message is absorbed into a large internal state and the digest is squeezed out of it. Both are hash functions, but they are built differently.</p>
<p>Ethereum was designed before SHA-3 became a standard and uses the original Keccak. NIST changed the padding byte when it standardised it (0x01 in Keccak, 0x06 in SHA3-256), so <b>Keccak-256 and SHA3-256 give different outputs for the same input</b>.</p>
<p class="mono" id="hcmp" style="overflow-wrap:anywhere"></p>

<h2>3. Wallet pipeline</h2>
<p>Mnemonic → (BIP-39) seed → (BIP-32 / BIP-44) private key → (secp256k1) public key → (Keccak-256) hash → last 20 bytes → (EIP-55) checksummed address.</p>
<div class="warn">For learning only. The test phrase is public, so never use it for real funds, and never type a real recovery phrase into a page like this.</div>
<label for="mn">Mnemonic (12 words) <textarea id="mn" rows="3" spellcheck="false"></textarea></label>
<div class="status" id="mnstatus"></div>
<label for="path">Derivation path <input type="text" id="path" value="m/44'/60'/0'/0/0"></label>
<p><button class="main" id="bgen" type="button">Generate random wallet</button> <button id="bder" type="button">Derive custom path</button> <button id="btest" type="button">Use test phrase</button></p>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Primitive</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
<tbody id="pipe"></tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted">Try the test phrase in MetaMask or Rabby. The first account should show the same address. Changing the last number of the path (…/0/1, …/0/2) gives the next accounts.</p>

<h2>4. Statistical exploration</h2>
<p>A good hash should show an avalanche effect: flipping one input bit should flip about half of the output bits. Here the input is the 64-byte public key of the wallet above. In each of 64 trials one bit of the key is flipped, the Keccak-256 digest is recomputed, and the two digests are compared.</p>
<p><button class="main" id="bstat" type="button">Run 64 trials</button></p>
<div id="statout"></div>
<h3>Other expectations (not tested here)</h3>
<ul>
<li>The PBKDF2 seed (512 bits) should look random, even though it comes from only 128 bits of entropy.</li>
<li>The private key lies between 1 and n − 1, where n ≈ 1.158 × 10<sup>77</sup>, so brute force is not possible.</li>
<li>In EIP-55 the capital letters follow the bits of a hash, so about half of the letters a–f are capitals.</li>
<li>A typo is missed by the EIP-55 checksum with probability around 2<sup>-15</sup> (about 0.003%).</li>
</ul>

<h2>5. Why several different techniques?</h2>
<p>One algorithm cannot do all of these jobs. Each step has a different goal:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>BIP-39, BIP-32, BIP-44</b> manage keys. One phrase is easy to back up, and gives many accounts in a standard layout that every wallet understands.</li>
<li><b>secp256k1</b> links the private and public key and allows digital signatures, so the owner can prove ownership without revealing the key.</li>
<li><b>Keccak-256</b> shortens the 64-byte public key to a 20-byte identifier, and the public key stays hidden until the first transaction.</li>
<li><b>EIP-55</b> detects typing errors in the address, without changing the address itself.</li>
</ul>
